#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# n_dsolve_motion

#% text
# `n_dsolve_motion(L, q, [t, t0, t1], q0, v0)` integrates the Euler-Lagrange equations of `L` numerically from the positions `q0` and velocities `v0` at `t0` to `t1`, and gives the rules of `n_dsolve` for the coordinate names.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The accelerations are solved from the equations first (the mass matrix may depend on the coordinates), so `n_dsolve` gets an explicit system; the parameters of `L` must have numeric values.

#% item
# Further arguments are options of `n_dsolve`.

#% item
# `energy_drift` measures how well a solution keeps the energy.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
s = n_dsolve_motion(th'(t)^2/2 + cos(th(t)), th, [t, 0, 20], 1, 0)

#% cell
rewrite(th(10.0), s)
#% output
#= -0.9989497740436374

#% text
# The double pendulum, and the change of its energy over the run:

#% cell
ld = (2*a'(t)^2 + b'(t)^2 + 2*a'(t)*b'(t)*cos(a(t) - b(t)))/2 + 9.81*(2*cos(a(t)) + cos(b(t)))

#% cell
s2 = n_dsolve_motion(ld, [a, b], [t, 0, 10], [1, 0.5], [0, 0])

#% cell
rewrite([a(10.0), b(10.0)], s2)
#% output
#= [-0.34218273311403347, -0.5558580052472979]

#% cell
energy_drift(ld, [a, b], s2, [t, 0, 10]) < 1.0e-5
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `energy_drift`, `euler_lagrange_equations`, `n_dsolve`, `phase_portrait`
